<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bal Gangadhar Tilak</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1856">1856</a>–<a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Lokmanya Tilak, B. G. Tilak</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bal Gangadhar Tilak, called Lokmanya, was an Indian nationalist and an early, forceful advocate of swaraj, self-rule, known for the slogan &quot;Swaraj is my birthright and I shall have it&quot;. He founded the weeklies Kesari and Mahratta and, with Lala Lajpat Rai and Bipin Chandra Pal, led the radical wing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a>, urging swadeshi and the boycott of foreign goods after the partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. Imprisoned for sedition at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a> from 1908 to 1914, where he wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Gita</a> Rahasya, he later helped found the All <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> Home Rule League.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bal-gangadhar-tilak.jpg" alt="Bal Gangadhar Tilak" width="320" height="472" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Internet Archive Book Images · No restrictions · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Young_India;_an_interpretation_and_a_history_of_the_nationalist_movement_from_within_(1916)_(14781987942).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tilak was born Keshav Gangadhar Tilak on 23 July 1856 in a Marathi Chitpavan Brahmin family in Ratnagiri; the family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Poona</a> in 1867. His father, a schoolteacher and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> scholar, died when Tilak was sixteen. Tilak took a degree in mathematics at the Deccan College in 1877 and a law degree in 1879. In 1880 he co-founded the New English school, and in 1884 the Deccan Education Society, which set up Fergusson College in 1885, where he taught mathematics. He left the society in 1890 for more openly political work. He had started the weeklies Kesari, in Marathi, and Mahratta, in English, in 1880 to 1881.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Tilak joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> in 1890 and opposed its moderate approach to self-rule. Measures against the plague in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> in 1897 caused widespread resentment, and Tilak published inflammatory articles in Kesari and Mahratta. After two British officers were shot by the Chapekar brothers he was charged and sentenced to 18 months in prison. He took up the slogan Swaraj is my birthright and I shall have it. After the Partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> he promoted the Swadeshi and boycott movements. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat</a> session of 1907 Congress split into a radical faction led by Tilak, Bipin Chandra Pal and Lala Lajpat Rai, and a moderate one.</p><p>In 1908 he was convicted of sedition over articles in Kesari and sentenced to six years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>, where he was held until 16 June 1914. He rejoined Congress at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a> pact of 1916 and helped found the All India Home Rule League, whose membership grew from 1400 in April 1916 to about 32,000 in 1917. He also turned the household worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganesha">Ganesha</a> into a public festival in 1894 and began celebrations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a>'s birthday in 1895, though these celebrations also contributed to religious tensions. He died on 1 August 1920.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In prison at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a> he wrote the Gita Rahasya, interpreting the Bhagavad Gita as justifying activism, which he called karma-yoga, against the dominant renunciate readings. Tilak supported self-rule without a complete break with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>. He opposed the age of consent bill and the first girls' high school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a>, and he refused to sign a petition against untouchability in 1918. He also wrote The Arctic Home in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, arguing that the Vedas were composed in the Arctic.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bal Gangadhar Tilak” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A more radical group, led by <b>Bal Gangadhar Tilak</b> in Maharashtra, Bipin Chandra Pal in Bengal and Lala Lajpat Rai in the Punjab, demanded swaraj, self-rule, and urged swadeshi, the use of Indian goods, together with the boycott of British ones and national education; Tilak’s declaration that swaraj…</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>B. R. Ambedkar criticised its stance on caste and called its defence of killing unethical, while Gandhi credited his commitment to ahimsa (non-violence) to the Gita, and Lala Lajpat Rai and <b>Tilak</b> read it as defending war when necessary.</blockquote><a href="/work/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>In modern times Swami Vivekananda, Netaji Subhas Chandra Bose, <b>Bal Gangadhar Tilak</b>, Mahatma Gandhi and many others used it to help inspire the Indian independence movement.</blockquote><a href="/work/mahabharata">Mahabharata · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Bal Gangadhar Tilak</b> argued in 1893 for a composition date of at least 6000–4000 BCE, and &quot;Out of India&quot; arguments link it with the Indus Valley civilisation; mainstream scholarship does not accept these theories.</blockquote><a href="/work/rigveda">Rigveda · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratnagiri">Ratnagiri</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=16.98,73.3&amp;year=1897" aria-label="Ratnagiri on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Bal Gangadhar Tilak"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6078 6041h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6078 6041h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=16.98,73.3&amp;year=1897">What was here in 1897? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
